# THE UNIFIED RELEASE ENVIRONMENT for the verify and build jobs (Step 1L).
#
# ONE resolution, not several. v2 installed requirements.lock and then a
# separate publish lock in sequence; the two disagreed on 9 pins, and the
# second install silently replaced the first — so the environment the suite
# ran in was not the environment either lock described. pip-compile resolves
# runtime, test, and build requirements together here, so a conflict is a
# hard resolution failure at lock time instead of a silent overwrite on a
# release runner.
#
# The runtime and test requirements are NOT duplicated here: they are read
# from pyproject.toml (with the `test` extra) by the regeneration command
# below, so this file can never drift from the package's own metadata.
#
# Locked for the runner the workflow uses: Ubuntu 24.04 / CPython 3.12.3.
# Regenerate ONLY inside a clean Linux environment — a Windows resolution
# evaluates platform markers differently and produces a different tree.
# The toolchain itself is pinned so the lock is reproducible:
#
#   python3.12 -m venv /tmp/olymp-lock
#   /tmp/olymp-lock/bin/python --version  # Python 3.12.3
#   /tmp/olymp-lock/bin/pip install 'pip==24.0' 'pip-tools==7.6.1'
#   /tmp/olymp-lock/bin/pip-compile --generate-hashes --allow-unsafe \
#       --strip-extras --extra test \
#       --output-file=requirements-publish.lock \
#       pyproject.toml requirements-publish.in
#
# `--allow-unsafe` pins setuptools: the workflow builds with
# `python -m build --no-isolation`, so the build backend
# (setuptools.build_meta, per pyproject's `requires`) must come from this
# hash-locked set rather than an unpinned network resolution at build time.

# Build and validation toolchain.
pip==24.0
build
twine
setuptools>=77
